// a `const ptr<int32>` promises only that it keeps pointing where it was seated. writing *through*
// it is allowed - the pointee is not const (book/concept/pointers_and_refs_v2.md, "Const")
//
// this used to be rejected. the const check lived in the parser and fired on the declared type's
// top level, which for a const pointer is the pointer itself, so it could not tell a write-through
// from a re-seat - the deref that distinguishes them is only inserted later, by the pointer
// adjustment pass. the check now runs there, on the assignment target's shape
$a = 1;

const ptr<int> $p = &$a;

$p = 5;

// the write landed in $a, because $p was never a copy of it
echo $a;
echo $p;

// the const pointee is the other half of the pair: that one may be re-seated, but not written
const int $c = 1;
const int $d = 2;

const int& $view = &$c;

$view:$ = &$d;

echo $view;
